welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is of shock Acharya an associate  professor and the Department of  Political Science and joint director of  developing countries Research Center at  the University of Delhi at Yale  professor Acharya is the Henry Hart Rice  foundation fellow at the Macmillan  Center he is author of the forthcoming  volume equality difference and group  writes the case of India professor  Acharya is research interests in compass  contemporary and comparative political  theory affirmative action and ethics and  public policy today we'll talk with him  about his project called know your  rights India  welcome professor Acharya thank you very  much that's a privilege so you are a  professor at the University of Delhi in  India why are you here at Yale as the  rice fellow well I was invited and you  know I thought I was just take up this  opportunity to be especially at Yale and  spend a year the invitation was pretty  exciting because it allowed me to work  with the Macmillan Center and especially  with the global justice program which is  where much of my current research  revolves around okay so let's talk about  your current project know your rights  India give us an overview of it know  your rights India is a project in its  infancy but this is a project that tries  to bring together on a wiki based  platform using online technologies  listing all the rights benefits and  teittleman's of all the people and  especially those that relate to the  needs and aspirations of the poor and  marginalized people of India and on one  single platform what we plan to do is  not only spit what all those rights are  of the people and the benefits are but  also sip you know give an account of a  simplified procedure of how to realize  those rights and it's not just about  literacy we also know that India doesn't  have full literacy that there is still a  substantial proportion of the people who  are illiterate what it would that  proportion there oh that would be  roughly around 40% would be illiterate  and so that's you know between 35 to 40%  and that's a huge number yes but when we  speak about the rights illiteracy per se  you know this this is a problem that we  see happening just anywhere it whether  you are literate or we are not literate  you know the rights and the language in  which those rights are expressed the  laws and the policies are not always  accessible to common people so people  have to struggle and that's why the  lawyers make a field day so what led you  to create this project what gave you the  idea well the idea was essentially to I  thought that we are right at a cusp in  India where we are foreseeing a  revolution of increasing recognition of  people social and economic rights so  there is a right to education you know  there is a right to employment and we  are soon debating you know very  seriously about the right to health  whereas I also felt that most of the  time and most of the people wouldn't  know what these rights are unless  somebody told them look these are your  entitlements and you can claim them and  that and and and and many of the NGOs  who work on the on this on these areas  are too specialized like you know there  are health NGOs and there are you know  livelihood related NGOs and they are  also you know geographically  concentrated in a few places so we felt  that if we have to build up in knowledge  community and through that empower the  people the best way to do is to build up  this the the the wiki based platform  which will not only educate people about  what the rights and entitlements are  the simplest of the language but also  bring in many of these NGOs and draw  them in into the network and through  them make them the interlocutors through  which we can speak to the common people  so it is about rights literacy campaign  sort of a rights education campaign  which also would need the help of the  NGOs right because as you had said  there's 35 to 40 percent of the country  is illiterate so they wouldn't  necessarily be able to read a website so  you would need that intermediary to work  with people absolutely absolutely  yes okay so what are the objectives well  the objectives are you know as I just  said you know the to build up a very  comprehensive website and in for each of  the right you know lead the person  through the various stages okay here is  your right in the most simplified  language and then if it is not realized  at this level you know you can take this  further up through this procedure so we  guide them step by step on every little  entitlement that they have and these can  vary by the way through across provinces  so different provinces would have  different ID teittleman's about how  there is to realize the power those  rights you know there are a set of  constitutional rights and there are  different sets of you know policy  entitlements that different provinces  would enact from time to time so is the  website in effect today could one go to  the website no it's not yet built up you  know we are we are not mobilizing all  our resources to build this up and when  it comes it will not be actually we  called know your rights it would be  called John a Dakar which means in Hindi  John means people and Alec are means  rights so people's rights okay so we  have registered the domain but we will  have to launch it right now at this  point of time we are collecting a lot of  data and especially at this stage we are  collecting data from four states and  India has many states you know 28 states  and so many union territories so we are  only collecting from four different  states geographically north south east  and west and in course of time we turn  this four different states into four  different nodal Jones so the northern  state  roxeanne for instance becomes the the  nodal zone for northern india and then  the eastern state of Orissa for instance  become the nodal zone for eastern India  and then they collect data from  neighboring states and and then amass  all of that okay so and you chose  Hindi because that's the most most  commonly spoken language that's roughly  forty percent of India's population  speaks Hindi though it is it is a it is  the it is a most spoken language in  India though there are other languages  and that's one of our father' challenges  that at the first step required  translate all the web content from  English to Hindi and then as a second  step we translate them into all the  other existing Indian languages as well  so more people have access to more  people have access so when do you think  it will launch oh that's a very very  tough question at this point of time  yeah we are we are we are trying our  best to have this launched as far as  their you know as soon as possible  but this means that you know working  closely with lots of institutions  educational institutions you know the  the law of faculties in the different  countries and I mean touch with some of  them to convert their legal clinics into  know your rights clinics as well as so  and I also want the content writing to  be done by the students so we have a  huge number of student interns working  on this and they have just begun the  work and only on these four states and  we hope that by end of July or August we  would be done with the work of these  four states after which we could  multiply and add the remainder of the  states and should be able to a  conservative estimate would be at least  you know two years from now and it could  be sooner you know I would be you know  but I'd be surprised if it is sooner  than that and once it is up and running  how you measure its success yeah that's  that's a very good question because  often enough we don't give enough you  know importance to this particular area  and so how do we measure  success and the the net behavior of any  net citizen you know is it's not always  very predictable people can suffer from  side to side so what we hope to be doing  is we measured the success of this on  the basis of three things of course the  first is very important is impact does  has it you know close the gap between  the between the level of people's  knowledge of what their formal  entitlements are and how they go about  realizing them so one of the ways of  measuring the success would be after a  given period of time if the if the rate  of benefits take up for any particular  right of some of the rights that are  highlighted in this website goes up then  we know we have had some success and but  that would be difficult to measure  because that would take a longer time in  the meantime we propose to you know do a  kind of a web analytics study on the on  the effectiveness of the content and use  some sample surveys to to measure as to  whether or not this is really appealed  to the people and whether they would  vote for it or whether they would  suggest certain alternatives to the  creation or the design of the of the  website and how they would what sorts of  suggestions they would give to improvise  the content of it and and the traffic so  the more number of hits of course we  mean we know that this is a success and  how would you how are you planning to  spread the word about the site so people  know about it this is this is something  we propose doing through the through the  educational institutions on the one hand  and also mostly through the NGOs whom we  are now trying to partner so I'd write  at this point of time what we have done  is we have almost successfully mapped  all the NGOs in the country Wow almost  all the NGOs in the thousands but we  have been able to map all of them and  now we are trying to  you know again categorize them as to how  effective they are in particular regions  and what are their areas of strength so  depending on which region which NGO  works in and what their areas of  strength we would further you know like  to open up an Avenue through which we  train even the NGOs to take up this task  at our behest because we can't be  reaching out everywhere sure right so we  will have to bring in the NGOs train  them and help them become the effective  interlocutors so so so the NGO mapping  has been one of the most challenging  exercises so far but it is still  something that you can do at home you  can surf the net and you can find out so  there's no central registry of NGOs in  India you know they get registered and  you have you know you can always get  information about these NGOs from the  different governments of the provinces  and the states but at times they can be  misleading also because you know many  organizations get registered and they  don't do anything right so there are the  there are the front organizations for  someone and so we want to find out not  only those organizations that are  registered but which are actually  working for the people and that's a  tough - and that's a tougher journey so  in which case we have to go through in a  step-by-step process so we not only do  we get the NGOs mapped but then we get  the entire info information vetted by  slightly bigger organizations and NGOs  which are in the business of funding  some of these local NGOs to find out  from them if you know we are right about  mapping out these NGOs as the effective  ones and whether they are really doing  the work in the in those areas seems  like a huge undertaking  it is ultimately what do you hope to  achieve with the project  oh very many things of course you know I  would say two things  primarily a I would say because of my  work on global justice and  I have become more sensitive to the and  then there's also a bunch of scholars  who are supporting me in this work back  in India we have become very sensitive  to the needs of the poor and the  marginalized so the first big goal that  I think we should be able to achieve is  still you have a better sense of how  this website is going to enhance the  livelihoods of people and that I think  would be a big achievement  the second big achievement I would say  is to create a knowledge community bring  academics in in closer contact with  activists and have a richer knowledge  community out there which then goes you  know goes out to strengthen our  démocratique experience enrich our  citizen engagement at different levels  and also somewhere pressurizes the  government to be more responsive to the  needs of the people and so we can you  know as academics we can keep theorizing  as to what makes democracy work but it's  it this would be a different kind of a  challenge to go out and see how do we  actually make democracy work very good  well thank you so much for being here  with us today and I wish you all the  best with the project oh thank you so  much it's a real pleasure for more  information about Professor Acharya and  his research please visit our website at  Yale edu slash macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale

